\title{Large Language Models Can Automatically Engineer Features for Few-Shot Tabular Learning}

\begin{abstract}
[S]Large Language Models (LLMs), with their remarkable ability to tackle challenging and unseen reasoning problems, hold immense potential for tabular learning, that is vital for many real-world applications.[E] [S]In this paper, we propose a novel in-context learning framework, \textsf{FeatLLM}, which employs LLMs as feature engineers to produce an input data set that is optimally suited for tabular predictions.[E] [S]The generated features are used to infer class likelihood with a simple downstream machine learning model, such as linear regression and yields high performance few-shot learning.[E] [S]The proposed \textsf{FeatLLM} framework only uses this simple predictive model with the discovered features at inference time.[E] [S]Compared to existing LLM-based approaches, \textsf{FeatLLM} eliminates the need to send queries to the LLM for each sample at inference time.[E] [S]Moreover, it merely requires API-level access to LLMs, and overcomes prompt size limitations.[E] [S]As demonstrated across numerous tabular datasets from a wide range of domains, \textsf{FeatLLM} generates high-quality rules, significantly (10\% on average) outperforming alternatives such as TabLLM and STUNT.[E]
